p=pathlib.Path(__file__).parent;f=p/'candidate-brothers-karamazov-modern-en.json';d=json.loads(f.read_text());r=json.loads((p/'ENGLISH-REPAIRS.json').read_text());c=d['chapters'][84]
texts={16:'“But here is the third son of the modern father of a family,” Ippolit Kirillovitch continued. “He sits in the dock before us. His exploits, his life and deeds are before us too. The time has come, and everything has unfolded, everything has been exposed. In contrast to his brothers’ ‘Europeanism’ and ‘principles of the people,’ he seems to represent spontaneous Russia—oh, not all Russia, not all, and God forbid that it should be all! Yet here she is, our dear little Russia; you can smell her, hear her, our mother! Oh, we are spontaneous, we are good and evil in the most extraordinary mixture; we love enlightenment and Schiller, and at the same time we riot in taverns and tear the beards from our drunken companions. Oh, we too can be good and noble, but only when things are good and pleasant for ourselves. Indeed, we are overwhelmed—positively overwhelmed—by the noblest ideals, but only on condition that they are achieved by themselves, that they fall from heaven onto our table, and above all that they come free, free, that we pay nothing for them. We hate paying, but we love receiving, and that is so in everything. Oh, give us, give us every good thing in life—every one of them, we will settle for no less—and above all do not thwart our whims, and then we too will prove that we can be good and noble. We are not greedy, no, but give us money all the same, more, more, as much money as possible, and you will see how generously, with what contempt for the vile metal, we scatter it in a single night of unbridled revelry. And if we are not given money, we will show how we can get it when we badly want it. But more of that later; let us follow the events in order.',19:'“What are we to believe? The first story—the impulse of lofty generosity that gives away the last means of living and bows before virtue—or the other, revolting side of the medal? Ordinarily, when there are two opposites, one must seek the truth between them; in this case that is literally not so. Most likely he was sincerely noble in the first instance and just as sincerely base in the second. Why? Precisely because we are broad natures, Karamazov natures—that is what I am leading up to—capable of containing every kind of contradiction and contemplating both abysses at once: the abyss above us, the abyss of the highest ideals, and the abyss below us, the abyss of the lowest, foulest degradation. Remember the brilliant thought expressed earlier by a young observer who has studied the whole Karamazov family closely and deeply, Mr. Rakitin: ‘The sense of the baseness of their fall is as necessary to these unbridled, unrestrained natures as the sense of the highest nobility.’ And it is true: they need precisely this unnatural mixture, constantly and without interruption. Two abysses, two abysses, gentlemen, at one and the same moment—without that we are unhappy and dissatisfied, our existence incomplete. We are broad, broad as our whole mother Russia; we can contain everything and live with everything!'}
